WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.118
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:09.013
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.313 --> 00:00:15.785
Unit thirteen: Systems Thinking. Today you will describe analysis using searching for / looking for + noun.

4
00:00:16.085 --> 00:00:18.540
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.524
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:24.768
Systems thinking.

7
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:29.559
Systems thinking means understanding how parts interact within a whole.

8
00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.427
Systems thinking reveals why quick fixes often backfire.

9
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:36.750
Your turn. Say: Systems thinking.

10
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.961
Systems thinking.

11
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:42.567
Feedback.

12
00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:47.603
Feedback means information about results that influences later action.

13
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:50.913
Negative feedback keeps a thermostat stable.

14
00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:54.018
Your turn. Say: Feedback.

15
00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:57.717
Feedback.

16
00:00:58.483 --> 00:00:59.554
Interconnection.

17
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.361
Interconnection means a link between things so that each affects the other.

18
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.006
The interconnection of banks spread the crisis worldwide.

19
00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:11.223
Your turn. Say: Interconnection.

20
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.219
Interconnection.

21
00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:17.205
Holistic view.

22
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:22.747
Holistic view means an approach that considers the whole rather than isolated parts.

23
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.545
A holistic view of health includes sleep and community.

24
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:30.046
Your turn. Say: Holistic view.

25
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.191
Holistic view.

26
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.218
Leverage point.

27
00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:41.712
Leverage point means a place in a system where a small change produces large effects.

28
00:01:42.317 --> 00:01:45.593
Changing incentives was the leverage point for reform.

29
00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:49.138
Your turn. Say: Leverage point.

30
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.245
Leverage point.

31
00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:55.971
Unintended consequence.

32
00:01:56.983 --> 00:02:00.875
Unintended consequence means an unplanned result of an action.

33
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:05.782
The ban had an unintended consequence: a black market.

34
00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:09.651
Your turn. Say: Unintended consequence.

35
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.418
Unintended consequence.

36
00:02:15.183 --> 00:02:16.080
Boundary.

37
00:02:17.083 --> 00:02:21.022
Boundary means the limit that defines what is inside or outside a system.

38
00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:24.371
Where you draw the boundary changes what you can see.

39
00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:27.299
Your turn. Say: Boundary.

40
00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:31.088
Boundary.

41
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.443
Dynamic equilibrium.

42
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.236
Dynamic equilibrium means a balance maintained by continuous opposing processes.

43
00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:44.160
A healthy lake sustains dynamic equilibrium through constant exchange.

44
00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.436
Your turn. Say: Dynamic equilibrium.

45
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.009
Dynamic equilibrium.

46
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.555
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:58.417 --> 00:03:02.602
Visal, the city widened the highway, yet traffic got worse. Why?

48
00:03:03.717 --> 00:03:11.176
Systems thinkers search for the right leverage point rather than the obvious one. More lanes invited more cars.

49
00:03:12.283 --> 00:03:14.248
So the fix created the problem?

50
00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:25.212
In part. An unintended consequence often follows when planners look for a quick solution inside a narrow boundary.

51
00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:28.130
What should they have searched for?

52
00:03:29.217 --> 00:03:38.313
They should have been searching for the underlying feedback: why people drive. Better transit, not wider roads, may restore equilibrium.

53
00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:41.176
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.017 --> 00:03:45.177
Grammar focus: Searching for and looking for plus noun.

55
00:03:45.477 --> 00:03:46.676
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.383 --> 00:03:50.219
Systems thinkers search for the right leverage point.

57
00:03:51.117 --> 00:03:53.824
Planners are looking for sustainable solutions.

58
00:03:54.717 --> 00:03:57.750
Analysts should have been searching for the underlying feedback.

59
00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:08.054
search for / look for + noun (not 'search the', which means to examine a place). 'Search the house for keys' but 'search for keys'. Both can be progressive or perfect.

60
00:04:08.817 --> 00:04:12.816
Be careful! Don't say: Thinkers search the right leverage point.

61
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:17.800
Say: Thinkers search for the right leverage point.

62
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.519
search for + noun.

63
00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:23.055
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.867 --> 00:04:24.984
Seeing the Whole.

65
00:04:25.284 --> 00:04:32.183
Systems thinking is the discipline of understanding how parts interrelate within a whole, rather than studying them in isolation.

66
00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:40.925
Its practitioners search for feedback structures and leverage points, places where a modest intervention can shift the behaviour of an entire system.

67
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:51.654
Ignoring a system's boundaries, by contrast, often leads to unintended consequences: a policy solves a local problem and creates a larger one elsewhere.

68
00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:59.484
Donella Meadows argued that the most powerful leverage points lie in the goals and mindsets of a system, not in its numbers.

69
00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.634
A holistic view does not eliminate surprise, but it makes surprise less frequent and less costly.

70
00:05:06.733 --> 00:05:10.793
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:14.671
Systems thinkers search blank the right leverage point.

72
00:05:14.971 --> 00:05:17.522
A, for. B, of. C, at.

73
00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:22.892
The answer is A: for.

74
00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.909
An unplanned result of an action is an blank.

75
00:05:27.209 --> 00:05:32.313
A, unintended consequence. B, interconnection. C, equilibrium.

76
00:05:35.867 --> 00:05:38.644
The answer is A: unintended consequence.

77
00:05:39.783 --> 00:05:42.897
A place where a small change has large effects is a blank.

78
00:05:43.197 --> 00:05:47.002
A, boundary. B, leverage point. C, feedback.

79
00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:52.880
The answer is B: leverage point.

80
00:05:54.017 --> 00:05:55.772
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:56.072 --> 00:06:02.361
A, She searched for the cause.. B, She searched of the cause.. C, She searched at for the cause..

82
00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.677
The answer is A: She searched for the cause..

83
00:06:09.817 --> 00:06:13.674
According to the reading, the most powerful leverage points lie in…

84
00:06:13.974 --> 00:06:18.556
A, numbers only. B, goals and mindsets. C, random events.

85
00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:25.121
The answer is B: goals and mindsets.

86
00:06:26.333 --> 00:06:29.508
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:34.346
What is a 'leverage point' and why is it valuable in systems thinking?

88
00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.650
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:52.950 --> 00:07:21.547
A leverage point is a place in a system where a small, well-chosen change produces a large effect. It is valuable because systems resist brute force; pushing hard at the wrong place often fails or backfires. Systems thinkers therefore search for the point where feedback can be redirected. Changing incentives can matter more than adding resources. Finding it takes patience and a holistic view.

90
00:07:21.847 --> 00:07:23.638
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:29.199
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and searching for and looking for plus noun.

92
00:07:29.499 --> 00:07:34.346
Today's value is holistic thinking. Look at the whole picture, not only one piece.

93
00:07:34.646 --> 00:07:37.542
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:37.842 --> 00:07:40.657
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

